 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Real-time KD Tree Construction](4437-real-time-kd-tree-construction.md)
+Real-time KD Tree Construction is a technique for building k-d tree spatial partitioning structures on the GPU to enable immediate use in rendering pipelines. It sets itself apart from its parent method, [K-D Tree Hierarchy Construction](2300-k-d-tree-hierarchy-construction.md), by utilizing GPU parallelism and breadth-first search (BFS) to achieve real-time performance. It differs from [GPU K-D Tree Ray Traversal](4575-gpu-k-d-tree-ray-traversal.md) by focusing on the *construction* phase rather than the *traversal* phase [G].
+
+### [GPU K-D Tree Ray Traversal](4575-gpu-k-d-tree-ray-traversal.md)
+GPU K-D Tree Ray Traversal accelerates ray tracing on graphics hardware by intersecting rays with a hierarchical spatial partitioning structure (K-D tree). It efficiently skips empty space and identifies intersected primitives, avoiding the computational cost of testing every primitive in the scene. It is distinguished from CPU implementations by its adaptation to GPU architectures: early Shader Model 2.0 hardware used stackless multi-pass streaming kernels, while Shader Model 3.0 introduced single-pass traversal via dynamic branching. It builds on [K-D Tree Hierarchy Construction](2300-k-d-tree-hierarchy-construction.md).
+
+### [Parallel K-d Tree Construction](5421-parallel-k-d-tree-construction.md)
+Parallel K-d Tree Construction is a cooperative parallel partitioning process that uses a k-d tree to group data cells into local regions, restoring spatial ordering to allow early ray merging and efficient rendering in distributed-memory systems. It sets itself apart from static partitioning methods by dynamically determining split planes via global consensus and managing neighbor links to handle boundary conditions, ensuring that processors render cells in the same neighborhood at approximately the same time.
+
+### [K-D Tree Subdivision](8148-k-d-tree-subdivision.md)
+A data partitioning method using a k-D tree structure with alternating binary subdivision of coordinate planes at each level, used when the viewpoint is unknown or perspective projection is used.
+
 ### Axis Selection via Variance
-Selects the splitting axis by analyzing the variance of the QEF (Quadratic Error Function) solution along each dimension, scaled by the box size [S2, unofficial]. It solves the QEF to get an approximate solution and error, then calculates variance for each axis: $variance[i] = qef.getVariance(approximate) \times size[i]$ [S2, unofficial]. It selects the axis with maximum variance. If the size of the max-variance axis is less than 2, it falls back to $3 - maxVarDir - minVarDir$, and if that size is also less than 2, it falls back to the min-variance axis [S2, unofficial]. This is used in contexts like KdtreeISO [S2, unofficial].
-
-### Parallel K-d Tree Construction
-A cooperative parallel partitioning process where all processors participate in a synchronized partitioning after initial data distribution [S3]. A designated host node receives proposed local cutting positions from each processor, averages them to obtain a global cutting position, and broadcasts this position along with the next subregion to be partitioned [S3]. Cells intersecting the cut boundary are assigned to the region containing their centroid [S3]. It is preferred over octrees because it supports adaptive partitioning along orthogonal coordinate planes and allows straightforward determination of depth ordering [S3]. It does not guarantee a well-balanced tree, but the searching overhead is insignificant compared to rendering time [S3].
+Axis Selection via Variance selects the splitting axis for a K-D tree node by analyzing the variance of the QEF (Quadratic Error Function) solution along each dimension, scaled by the box size [S2, unofficial]. The steps are: 1. Solve QEF to get an approximate solution and error. 2. Calculate variance for each axis: $variance[i] = qef.getVariance(approximate) \times size[i]$. 3. Find $maxVarDir$ (index of max variance) and $minVarDir$ (index of min variance). 4. Set $dir = maxVarDir$. If $size[maxVarDir] < 2$, fallback to $3 - maxVarDir - minVarDir$. If that size $< 2$, fallback to $minVarDir$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ for balanced trees, where $N$ is the number of polygons. Construction is $O(N \log N)$ if splitting is efficient (e.g., median finding). Round-robin splitting is simple but may not produce optimal balance [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing polygons and tree nodes. The flat array `Tid` avoids linked list overhead [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Extends to about 16 processors before contention becomes a serious drawback in parallel implementations [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids explicit polygon clipping; efficient for spatial queries; supports adaptive partitioning [S1, S3].
-*   **Limitations:** May produce unbalanced trees if data distribution is skewed or if splitting strategy is poor [S3]. Parallel construction requires synchronization [S3].
+- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. Standard k-d tree construction is $O(N \log N)$ on average, but the source does not specify this for the volume rendering context [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** The use of a flat `Tid` array and prefix-order storage avoids the overhead of linked lists, reducing memory usage compared to naive implementations [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Avoids explicit polygon clipping; efficient subtree traversal via contiguous memory access in `Tid`; scalable to multiple processors (up to ~16 before contention becomes a drawback) [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Round-robin splitting may not be optimal for all data distributions; variance-based selection adds computational cost [S1][S2, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When rendering irregular grids or multiple grids where spatial locality is important and explicit clipping is expensive [S1].
+- **When not to use:** When real-time construction is required on GPU without parallel algorithms [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The k-d tree was introduced by Jon Bentley in 1975 [S1]. It has been used in direct volume rendering for irregular and multiple grids [S1]. The method of storing polygon IDs in prefix-order traversal order in a common array was developed to avoid the extra space of linked lists [S1].
+The k-d tree was introduced by Jon Bentley [1]. The method described in the primary source is part of a hierarchical and parallelizable direct volume rendering approach for irregular and multiple grids [S1]. It was developed to address the need for efficient spatial partitioning in volume rendering, specifically to avoid the complexity of scanline-based polygon grouping [S1].
 
